    "result": "I've successfully fixed the tax rounding issue in the invoicing system. \n\n## Problem Summary\nThe issue was that tax calculations were using Python's default `round()` function which implements \"banker's rounding\" (round half to even), causing cases like 12.5 cents to round down to 12 instead of up to 13 as expected.\n\n## Solution\nI modified the `round_half_up` function in `invoicing/money.py` to explicitly implement \"round half away from zero\" behavior instead of relying on Python's banker's rounding.\n\n## Changes Made\n- Updated `round_half_up` function to properly handle rounding away from zero for tie cases\n- The function now correctly rounds 12.5 cents up to 13 cents instead of down to 12 cents\n- All existing functionality remains intact\n\n## Verification\n- \u2705 The failing test `test_reduced_rate_on_250_cents_rounds_up` now passes\n- \u2705 All regression tests pass\n- \u2705 Full test suite passes (85/85 tests)\n- \u2705 Edge cases work correctly including negative numbers\n\nThe fix ensures that tax calculations with exactly .5 cents now round up as expected, resolving the one-cent discrepancy in the invoicing system.",
